wlanpi-core (2.3.22-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Track the interfaces Core creates (by namespace, name and ifindex, on
    tmpfs) and make the default configuration touch only those and radios
    in namespaces Core created. Interfaces from drivers or other tools, such
    as wlanpi-profiler's hostapd, are left alone and reported as skipped;
    after a reboot the default is a no-op (#202).
  * Deactivate restores its own root entries (the interface name, managed)
    instead of applying the default afterwards, and a failed activation only
    records default (#288, #271).
  * A revert step that fails is reported instead of leaving a radio without
    an interface, and a failure after prepare (e.g. wpa_supplicant not
    starting) puts that entry back.
  * Namespace markers record the namespace's identity, so a same-named
    namespace created by another tool is not taken for Core's, and a marker
    is only removed once its namespace is gone (#275).

 -- Ben Toner <ben@numerousnetworks.co.uk>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 04:11:19 -0500

wlanpi-core (2.3.21-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Make the deprecated POST /network/wlan/revert do what it says and say
    what it does (#274): it reverts everything Core manages (tears down the
    active configuration, returns every Core namespace to root, and makes
    the default configuration active, updating current.txt). iface,
    namespace and delete_namespace are ignored, as now documented; the
    response no longer claims a single interface was reverted.

